From: hhuang@MIT.EDU
Subject: POLI/LEGAL: discrimination laws

I had posted,

>I must mention yet another protected category, age, as covered by the
>Age Disabilities Act of 1967.

That should be the ``Age Discrimination Act of 1967.''

>In 1991, Congress gave sexual harassment victims the right to a jury
>trial and the right to sue for back pay compensation.

This was under the 1991 Civil Rights Act, yet another one of many
statist bills that George Bush signed.  It makes for another good
example of rights expansion: Title VII of the 1964 Civil Rights Act
legislated the right to be free from discrimination with respect to
``compensation, terms, conditions, or privileges of employment.''  But
under the 1991 Civil Rights Act, this expanded into having the right
to sue for emotional damages under all federally protected categories
of discrimination.  This fanned the liability-driven workplace
censorship that we see today.

Remember that the 1986 _Meritor_ Court reaffirmed that sexual
*harassment*, under the hostile environment clause, is actionable as
sexual *discrimination*.  It is crucial to not let the surface issue
of harassment muddy up discussions.  You want to go after the root
cause: discrimination laws.

On to Martin Minow's post.  First, the technical points,

>It is my understanding that civil trials (which would be generally
>appropriate for harassment cases) do not result in jail sentences.
>Furthermore, civil trials are decided "on a preponderance of the
>evidence" while criminal trials require the higher standard of "beyond
>a reasonable doubt."

Precisely my point.  We need not even prove that the defendant
commited ``beyond a reasonable doubt'' a crime of ``harassment.''  We
only need a ``preponderance of the evidence'' that the company took
inadequate steps towards restricting harassing conduct.  This proven,
the plaintiff can collect hundreds of thousands in the federal courts,
or upwards of millions in state courts, depending on how sympathetic
juries are to claims of emotional damage.

>>Merely doing nothing may not be good enough in court...
>
>This was established, as I recall, in a case in New Hampshire where an
>individual was harassed and complained to management who chose to ignore
>the problem. The fact that management was aware of the problem and
>explicitly let the harassment continue was key: it was more than just
>"doing nothing."

There are many cases of sexual harassment which would prove what I
said.  However, let me give you one example here at Massachusetts.  In
1992, an MIT employee filed a suit under the Massachusetts Commission
Against Discrimination that contained three charges, one of which
claimed MIT didn't do enough to stop harassment, though in fact, MIT
at the time had a harassment policy that was even broader than both
the University of Michigan and University of Wisconsin policies thrown
out by federal district courts respectively in 1989 and 1991 on First
Amendment grounds (note that these are public universities; MIT is a
private university).

So, as you see, doing something or even doing a lot may not be good
enough in court.

Discrimination and harassment laws are particularly dangerous because
they result in liability-driven censorship that go far beyond the
wording of the original statutes.  For example, given that the average
sexual harassment jury award is a quarter of a million dollars, what
does one expect companies to do?

In the rest of his post, Martin implies that these laws are reasonable
and just.  He cites the example of Boston banks engaging in
``red-lining.''  But most tellingly, he writes,

>There are a few jobs that cannot safely be carried out by an extremely
>obese individual; excepting those, an obese individual should have the
>same right and opportunity to earn a living and, in doing so, to work
>in an environment free from harassment, as someone of normal weight.

Martin, to attack on the Extropians List the utility of core
libertarian ideas, such as private property and freedom of
association, is bad enough.  But to assert that people have a right to
be free from discrimination (once again, harassment is actionable
because it is discrimination) goes way overboard.  You either have not
grasped or refuse to acknowledge Libertarianism 101.  Do reread the
list charter and Extropian principles.

I also highly recommend reading David Friedman's _The Machinery of
Freedom: Guide to a Radical Capitalism_ and Bruce Benson's _The
Enterprise of Law: Justice Without the State_ -- even for committed
libertarians among us.

-Han Y. Huang                                           hhuang@mit.edu

