I've stayed out of this debate so far, but I think that there are
a lot of misunderstandings floating around, so I figure I'll add
a little to them if I can!  Before I do, I would like to sincerely
wish happy "holidays" to Extropians everywhere; although there's
no particular reason to emphasize such thoughts at this time of
year, I am always emotionally affected by the coming of a new
year and all of the wonderful promise it holds.  I wish you all
the best (and drive carefully!).

That said, I respond to this message of Tim's:

> > Nancy: 
> > > I am not arguing that Tim is likely to go on a physical rampage against
> > > the poor. On the other hand, I do think that a sense of empathy or a
> > > feeling that other people are of the same "kind" as oneself is part
> > > of what prevents mass murder, 
> 
> So, I am now being compared to mass murderers, or is this a
> continuation of the flames of Price and Starr?

No, I don't think so; in fact, it looks to me like she was trying to
carefully make sure that you didn't get that impression.  Rather, she's
saying that beyond the calculational reasons involving probabilities
of violent consequences, we don't go around murdering people who offend
us because we understand that they are like us in important ways.  Tim
has himself said exactly this -- forcefully, and multiple times, by
saying that others can do what they like, just as he can.  He doesn't
just argue that only he be exempt from statist coercion, but that
everybody should -- and what possible reason could he have for arguing
thusly unless there was some empathy there?  Put another way, he
doesn't go out killing the human "garbage" because he doesn't want
to.  Why not?

> This does not
> mean I am "against" memetic inspection, just that I think Extropianism
> is not likely to be of great popularity....and there are better ways
> to change the world anyway.)

I think this is largely correct, though others whose reputation is
tied more closely to the expansion of something explicitly called
"Extropianism" will of course disagree, as it is in their interest
to do so.  On the other hand, a version of "Extropianism" as presented
in the Wired blurb could conceivably gain some popularity ("gee, ain't
the future great?  There's nothing we can't accomplish!"), but probably
not much.  As far as I can tell, for a "movement" to gain popularity
it has to be sharply focused on providing some specific vision of
how to give more of the "pie" to its constituents.  This is why
various wealth-redistribution by force "movements" have tended to
succeed -- there's always lots of impoverished that want what
the wealthy have.

Now, there would seem to be some hope in the fact that we are all
impoverished in terms of freedoms and even goods by the intrusion
of the State; but the sad fact is that most people see the State
as acting in their interest by giving them stuff they wouldn't
have otherwise.  I think that Tim Starr has a point that it should
be possible to disabuse people of this notion, but Extropianism
is hardly likely to be the catalyst for that; the Libertarian
Party has been trying for a long time to do just that, with some
success (not at the polls, but -- as has been pointed out -- in the
way that the popular mind seems to be turning away from regulation
as the cure for all ills.  Not as much as we'd like, but in some
significant ways, and with some significant exceptions (gun control
and environmentalism, but note how the environmental movement has
been nearly completely silent lately).

The problem with Extropianism as a movement is that it's not really
very sharply focused.  The one potential I think it has for achieving
"movement" status is by replacing religious memes with more concrete
promises of technological salvation.  This has the trememdous benefit
that technology is always advancing, but competing memesets do not
have the constant parade of miracles to support them.  I think Max
at least understands this quite well, and sees the problems in achieving
the necessary decoupling of dogma from transmigration.  By adding
transhumanism to traditional ideas about freedom, there's some real
potential for acquiring a base of support.  To me, this is exactly
what Extropianism is all about, and diversions into anti-ecofreak
rhetoric and so on are off-focus and not too helpful.

Also, there are inherent problems in any "movement" that champions
freedom and critical thought.  So be it.

> > Nick Szabo:
> > When society can shoot back with
> > _force majeure_, good manners towards society are required, even if 
> > that means being excrutiatingly politically correct.  Don't dis
> > the taxman.  Don't get the taxman's stooges all in a lather.
> 
> Fuck that, Nick, with all due respect to your many fine comments over
> the time I have known you. We don't have to be polite, show good
> manners, or be politically correct. You are of course free to do so.

It depends on what one is trying to accomplish.  An old bit of "wisdom"
says that when in Rome, you do what the Romans do if you want to thrive
there.  I don't see you condemning Intel for its government contracts
or Apple for its explicitly PC policies with which it tries to lure
left-leaning employees and customers.  On one level, we certainly do
have to play the cards we're dealt, and evolutionary pressures favor
organisms that exploit the environment they're actually in, not the
environment they'd like to be in.  Of course, individuals who don't
need or want to exploit those aspects of the "market" we all live in --
either because they're willing to forego the benefits of such
exploitation or because they're content with narrower niches in
which those market forces don't exert any real pressure -- can do
so.  I think Nick was making the broader point about the practical
advantages of paying your taxes and observing other Roman customs.

> I am not arguing
> for _more_ politics, I am arguing--as Duncan Frissell and others have
> argued--that politics is a peripheral issue, that political parties
> are useless, that the compelling changes will be technological.

I agree with this, certainly, but it doesn't explain the flare-up
of political discussion, which would simply die out if not fanned.

derek

